    "passageText": "[Academic Reading Passage 1]\nTHE BIO-ACOUSTICS OF DEEP-SEA CETACEAN COMMUNICATION\n\nIn the oligotrophic, lightless depths of the world's oceans, electromagnetic waves attenuate within meters, rendering optical sensory mechanisms virtually useless beyond the photic zone. Consequently, marine mammals—most notably odontocetes (toothed whales, including sperm whales and dolphins) and mysticetes (baleen whales, such as blue, fin, and humpback whales)—have evolved acoustic adaptations of extraordinary sophistication. Acoustic waves travel through saline seawater at approximately 1,500 meters per second, nearly four-and-a-half times faster than through ambient air, with minimal kinetic attenuation across thousands of nautical miles, particularly within the Deep Sound Channel, also known as the SOFAR (Sound Fixing and Ranging) channel. This horizontal layer of the ocean, typically located between depths of 600 and 1,200 meters, acts as an acoustic waveguide where the combination of temperature, salinity, and hydrostatic pressure creates a local minimum in the velocity of sound, trapping low-frequency vocalizations that refract back into the channel core without dissipating against the surface or seabed. Mysticete vocalizations, characterized by low-frequency infrasonic pulses between 10 and 40 Hertz, exploit the SOFAR channel to coordinate basin-wide transoceanic migration and seasonal reproductive displays. Conversely, odontocetes utilize specialized cranial organs to generate ultrasonic biosonar clicks for echolocation and prey capture. In the sperm whale (Physeter macrocephalus), the massive spermaceti organ—a lipid-filled reservoir situated above the upper jaw—coupled with the junk and parabolic frontal skull bone, acts as a biological acoustic lens. Pressurized air forced through phonic lips generates intense, directional clicks that can reach peak source levels of 236 decibels referenced to one micropascal. These acoustic impulses travel into abyssal trenches, bouncing off giant squid (Architeuthis dux) and returning to the whale's fat-filled acoustic window in the lower mandible, which conducts sound vibrations directly to the tympanoperiotic macromolecular auditory complex. However, anthropogenic ocean noise pollution from commercial cargo shipping propellers, seismic airgun arrays used in oil exploration, and low-frequency active military naval sonar poses catastrophic disruptions to cetacean bio-acoustics. Acoustic masking blinds odontocete echolocation foraging, while sudden acoustic trauma triggers panic decompression sickness, culminating in lethal mass stranding events along shallow coastal coastlines worldwide.",
