    "passageText": "[Academic Reading Passage 2]\nARCHITECTURAL SCALABILITY IN SUPERCONDUCTING QUANTUM PROCESSORS\n\nModern classical supercomputers, governed by Von Neumann architectures and semiconductor transistor scaling, face insurmountable physical barriers as gate oxides shrink toward single-digit nanometer dimensions, where quantum tunneling induces severe electrical leakage and thermal dissipation bottlenecks. Quantum computation circumvents these limitations by exploiting the fundamental principles of quantum mechanics: superposition and quantum entanglement. Whereas a classical bit represents a binary state of either 0 or 1, a quantum bit (qubit) can exist in a linear superposition of both basis states (|0⟩ and |1⟩) simultaneously, parameterized by complex amplitudes on the Bloch sphere: |ψ⟩ = α|0⟩ + β|1⟩, where |α|² + |β|² = 1. Among competing hardware modalities—including trapped ions, photonic circuits, neutral atom lattices, and nitrogen-vacancy diamond centers—superconducting transmons have emerged as the leading commercial architecture. A transmon qubit comprises a superconducting circuit patterned from thin-film niobium or aluminum on a high-purity silicon substrate, operating at cryogenic temperatures below 15 millikelvin inside a multi-stage dilution refrigerator. The critical non-linear inductive component is the Josephson junction—two superconducting electrodes separated by an ultra-thin insulating aluminum oxide barrier roughly one nanometer thick. Through Cooper pair tunneling, the Josephson junction creates an anharmonic energy spectrum, isolating the transition between the ground state |0⟩ and first excited state |1⟩ from higher harmonic excitations (|2⟩, |3⟩), enabling microwave pulse manipulation at 4 to 6 Gigahertz. However, maintaining quantum coherence remains an epic scientific challenge. Unwanted thermal photons, cosmic rays, magnetic flux noise, and dielectric surface defects induce two primary decoherence mechanisms: energy relaxation (characterized by time T1) and dephasing (characterized by time T2). In current state-of-the-art processors, coherence times hover around 100 to 300 microseconds, restricting gate operations to shallow depth circuits. To execute fault-tolerant quantum algorithms—such as Shor's algorithm for prime factorization or Grover's quantum search—processors must implement Quantum Error Correction (QEC). The leading protocol, the 2D surface code, entangles multiple physical data qubits with auxiliary syndrome qubits in a planar lattice to detect bit-flip (X) and phase-flip (Z) errors without collapsing the underlying superposition state. Achieving physical fault tolerance requires an error threshold below 0.1% per two-qubit gate, demanding thousands of physical qubits to encode a single pristine logical qubit.",
